/-!
# Tag 099F on the slices of a cover, for a sheaf of ideals of finite type

Let `(𝒞, 𝒪)` be a ringed site and let `ℐ ⊂ 𝒪` be a sheaf of ideals **of finite type**, which is
the hypothesis of [Stacks, Tag 099F]. Being of finite type means that some family of objects
`{U_i}` covering the site carries, on each member, finitely many sections of `ℐ` generating it.
This file shows that on each of those slices the conclusion of [Stacks, Tag 099F] holds: the
inclusion

`D_comp(𝒪_{U_i}, ℐ|_{U_i}) ⥤ D(𝒪_{U_i})`

has a left adjoint, namely the Čech completion of [Stacks, Tag 099B] at the restricted
generators.

Two things make this possible, both of which an earlier pass in this directory recorded as
missing. First, a section of `ℐ` over `U` *is* a global section of the localized structure
sheaf `𝒪_U` — `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.globalSectionOfSection` — because `Over.mk (𝟙 U)` is
a terminal object of `𝒞/U`, so finitely many sections of `ℐ` over `U` are finitely many *global*
sections on the slice and [Stacks, Tag 099B] applies to them. Second, and this is the content,
the sheaf of ideals they generate on the slice is `ℐ|_U` on the nose:
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.overIdeal_eq_idealOfGlobalSections_of_generates`. For `ℐ` itself
generated by finitely many global sections that is
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.overIdeal_idealOfGlobalSections` of
`OverIdealGlobalSections.lean`; here it is proved for a finite family of sections over `U`
generating `ℐ` in the sectionwise sense of `PresheafOfModules.Generates`.

## Main definitions

* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.globalSectionOfSection`: a section of `𝒪` over `U`, read as a
  global section of the localized structure sheaf `𝒪_U`.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.overIdeal_eq_idealOfGlobalSections_of_generates`: **a finite
  family of sections of `ℐ` over `U` which generates `ℐ` sectionwise generates `ℐ|_U` on the
  localized ringed site.**
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.exists_coversTop_isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete_overIdeal`:
  **[Stacks, Tag 099F] on the slices of a cover, for a sheaf of ideals of finite type** — there
  is a family of objects covering the site on each of whose slices the inclusion of the
  derived-complete objects has a left adjoint.

## Implementation notes

**Which finite-type condition.** Two spellings of [Stacks, Tag 03DL] part (5) are in play, and
the tree proves them equivalent: Mathlib's categorical `SheafOfModules.IsFiniteType`, and the
sectionwise `PresheafOfModules.IsFiniteType`. The theorem is proved from the sectionwise one,
whose data is exactly what the proof consumes — a covering family and, on each member, finitely
many sections of `ℐ` together with the statement that every section of `ℐ` on an object above it
is locally an `𝒪`-linear combination of their restrictions. The statement at Mathlib's
hypothesis, which is the tag's, is obtained from it by
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.presheaf_isFiniteType_of_isFiniteType` of `StacksFrontier/Algebra/Category/ModuleCat/Sheaf/SiteFiniteTypeBridge.lean`.

**What this does not do, and why.** It does not prove [Stacks, Tag 099F]. A reflector on each
slice of a cover is not a reflector on `𝒞`: `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.derivedCompletion` of
`ReflectorCompletion.lean` is `CategoryTheory.Functor.leftAdjoint`, an arbitrary choice of left
adjoint carrying no formula, pinned at best up to isomorphism by
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.exists_iso_derivedCompletion_of_bijective_precomp`; and objects of
a derived category do not glue along a cover from isomorphisms on the pieces. The source's own
proof does not glue them either — it globalizes one level down, on *complexes*, building a single
`K ⟶ 𝒪` in `D(𝒪)` as the sheafification of a presheaf of complexes, which is
[Stacks, Tags 099D and 099E], and then takes `E ↦ R𝑆heafHom_𝒪(K, E)` for the reflector. The
step from such a `K ⟶ 𝒪` to the reflector is `GlobalCechReflector.lean`, where all three
clauses of the universal property are checked on the slices of the covering family this file
produces; and the map `K ⟶ 𝒪` itself is [Stacks, Tag 099E],
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.exists_isGlobalExtendedCech`. The tag is then
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete` of `FiniteTypeReflector.lean`.

It is worth being exact about which locality is missing, since one of them is not. *Morphisms*
of `D(𝒪)` are local: a map is invertible as soon as its restriction to each slice of a covering
family is, by `Stacks.SheafOfModules.isIso_of_coversTop_mapDerivedCategory` of
`StacksFrontier/CategoryTheory/Sites/Cohomology/Slice/DerivedIsomorphismLocality.lean`. So if a
single global candidate `(Φ, η)` on `D(𝒪)` were in hand, both remaining clauses of the universal
property would be checkable on the slices of the covering family above, where
`exists_coversTop_isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete_overIdeal` says the Čech model applies —
`Φ` being `R𝑆heafHom_𝒪(K, -)` would restrict to the Čech completion of the slice by
[Stacks, Tag 08JB], `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.derivedHomRestriction`, together with the
isomorphism `K|_U ≅ Č(g)` that `IsGlobalExtendedCech` asserts. What was needed was not a locality
principle but the **candidate object** `K ⟶ 𝒪` itself, which is [Stacks, Tag 099E],
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.exists_isGlobalExtendedCech` of `FiniteTypeExtendedCech.lean`.

**Where the sections over `U` become global sections of the slice.** `globalSectionOfSection`
sends a section `a` of `𝒪` over `U` to the compatible family `V/U ↦ a|_V`; compatibility is
`CategoryTheory.Over.w`. Its value over `V/U` is `a|_V`, which is
`globalSectionVal_globalSectionOfSection`, and that identity is what carries the generation
statement across. No terminal object of `𝒞/U` is named, although that is the reason the
construction exists.

**Why the proofs are in term mode.** `(X.over U).ringSheaf` and `X.ringSheaf.over U` are the
same sheaf of rings only after `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.over` is unfolded, which the
elaborator does and the transparency at which `rw` checks its result does not; a `rw` whose
result crosses between the two spellings of a ring of sections is refused with an application
type mismatch. So every scalar computation here is either a `congrArg` with its motive's type
written out, or an `exact`.

